          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.455,
                0.439,
                0.545,
                0.461
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.471,
            0.902,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "**Remark :** You can verify that if one polygon is similar to another polygon and this second polygon is similar to a third polygon, then the first polygon is similar to the third polygon."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.535,
            0.902,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": "You may note that in the two quadrilaterals (a square and a rectangle) of Fig. 6.6, corresponding angles are equal, but their corresponding sides are not in the same ratio."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.604,
            0.472,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.498,
            0.603,
            0.815,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.455,
                0.782,
                0.543,
                0.802
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.6"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.819,
            0.902,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "So, the two quadrilaterals are not similar. Similarly, you may note that in the two quadrilaterals (a square and a rhombus) of Fig. 6.7, corresponding sides are in the same ratio, but their corresponding angles are not equal. Again, the two polygons (quadrilaterals) are not similar."
        },
